PART 3 — FINAL DATA OWNERSHIP

Every data type must have one clear authoritative source.

A. AniList

AniList is the authoritative source for available:

- Anime metadata.
- Movie metadata supported by AniList.
- Anime titles.
- Movie titles.
- Descriptions.
- Posters.
- Cover images.
- Banner images.
- Genres.
- Release information.
- Status.
- Season information.
- Episode counts when available.
- Search results.
- Media details.
- Other available catalog metadata.

Do not obtain catalog metadata from streaming providers.

Do not use the Backend as a fallback metadata provider.

Do not store Anime/Movie metadata, descriptions, posters, banners, or catalog search results in Firestore.

If AniList does not provide an individual episode title or description, use a clearly identified fallback such as "Episode 1", "Episode 2", etc. Never invent episode titles.

AniList does not guarantee complete per-episode metadata for every title. Handle missing information safely.

B. Firestore

Firestore stores shared streaming information:

- Provider discovery.
- Source discovery.
- Provider status.
- Source status.
- Stream URLs.
- URL expiry information.
- Quality.
- Codec.
- Audio language.
- Safe playback headers, where appropriate.
- Provider/source counts.
- Discovery status.
- Version information.
- Update timestamps.
- Validation information.

Season/Episode count summary fields may be stored as lightweight counters when required by the agreed schema, but do not store complete catalog metadata in Firestore.

C. Room

Room stores local data:

- AniList metadata cache.
- Poster/image references.
- Local search cache.
- Local Anime/Movie details.
- Local Season/Episode metadata.
- Shared streaming data cache.
- Cached Provider/Source records.
- Cached Stream URLs.
- Watch History.
- Watchlist.
- Preferences.
- Local synchronization versions.

Room must remain the primary local source for fast UI rendering.

D. Backend

The existing Backend is used only for:

1. Provider discovery when required Firestore discovery data is missing.
2. Source discovery when required Firestore source data is missing.
3. Direct Stream URL resolution when no playable cached URL is available.
4. Controlled re-resolution after cached playback failures.

Do not use Backend endpoints to retrieve catalog metadata, search results, posters, descriptions, or ordinary Anime/Movie details.

Preserve existing Backend API contracts.

