PART 9 — FIRESTORE SHARED CACHE AND MULTI-USER SYNCHRONIZATION

Firestore is shared between users.

If User A discovers a Provider or successfully resolves a Stream URL, other users should be able to reuse that shared information when it is valid and compatible.

Required behavior:

1. User A discovers missing Provider data.
2. Authorized system saves it to Firestore.
3. User B receives the updated discovery data.
4. User B reuses the saved Provider and Source records.
5. User B does not repeat discovery unnecessarily.
6. User B reuses a valid shared Stream URL.
7. If the URL is not playable for User B, try other cached Sources before controlled re-resolution.

9.1 Real-time listeners

Use Firestore snapshot listeners only for relevant streaming data.

Examples:

- Currently opened Anime Episode.
- Currently opened Movie.
- Active playback Source.
- Relevant discovery status.

Do not listen to the entire Anime catalog.

Do not listen to every Episode of every Anime.

Do not keep unnecessary listeners active after leaving a screen.

Remove listeners when the corresponding ViewModel or screen is no longer active.

9.2 Atomic distributed locking

Prevent multiple devices from resolving the same missing Source simultaneously.

Use an atomic distributed lease/lock with:

- Stable content ID.
- Provider ID.
- Source ID.
- Lock owner.
- Lock expiration.
- Resolve status.
- Recovery after process termination.

Use Firestore transactions where appropriate.

A lock must not remain permanently stuck if a device crashes.

Do not rely only on an in-memory Mutex because it cannot coordinate different users or devices.

9.3 Secure Firestore writes

Inspect existing Firestore rules and trusted write infrastructure.

Do not weaken security rules.

Do not allow arbitrary unauthenticated clients to publish globally shared URLs.

Use the existing authorized publishing path if one exists.

If the APK cannot securely publish shared URL updates with the existing infrastructure, report the exact limitation and required integration. Do not silently change Backend code, Cloud Functions, or security rules.

